course: dictionary
lesson: 180
title: Ahimsa: not harming living things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ahimsa is a noun from religion, and you may meet it in religious education or history. Context helps you work it out. Read the words around it and look for clues.
explain: «Ahimsa means non-violence to all living things; it is why many Hindus are vegetarian.» Non-violence means not using force or hurting others. The word «why» shows that the belief leads to a choice, so ahimsa is a belief about being gentle to all life.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «it is why many Hindus are gentle to all living things» still makes sense. A good meaning fits the sentence and does not change its message.
fact: Ahimsa means not harming any living thing, in thought, word or action.
fact: Ahimsa is an important teaching in Hinduism, Jainism and Buddhism.
fact: Ahimsa is also called non-violence, and it can be a way of life and a way of working for change.
fact: Mahatma Gandhi used ahimsa in India's struggle for freedom.
fact: You can say «the principle of ahimsa» or «practise ahimsa».
exercise: «Ahimsa means non-violence to all living things; it is why many Hindus are vegetarian.» What does ahimsa mean? | Eating plenty of vegetables for good health | Winning every argument | Keeping quiet in a library | *Not harming any living thing
exercise: Which religion teaches ahimsa? | Tuesday | Football | Algebra | *Jainism
exercise: Mahatma Gandhi used ahimsa in which struggle? | A school trip to the zoo | *India's struggle for freedom | A football match | Cooking a meal
test: What does the phrase «practise ahimsa» mean? | Practise a sport every day | *Live by not harming any living thing | Sing loudly in assembly | Learn a new language
test: «Jain monks may sweep the path so as not to step on insects.» Why might they sweep the path? | Because it is raining | *So they do not step on insects | To clean up litter for the school | To find lost coins
exam: «Ahimsa means non-violence to all living things; it is why many Hindus are vegetarian.» Why are many Hindus vegetarian? | Because they are ill | Because vegetables are cheaper | *Because ahimsa asks them not to harm living things | Because of a football match
